From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 00:52:20 +0200
Subject: arm64: dts: sophgo: mux the Milk-V Duo S microSD pads from the DT

The vendor U-Boot only muxes the SD0 bus pads to the SDIO0 function when it
finds a card in the slot at probe time; with the slot empty it muxes them to
XGPIO, applies pull-downs, and opens the slot's power switch
(cvi_sdhci_probe() in drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c of the vendor tree).
Linux inherited that state unchanged - the sdhci0 node carried no pinctrl -
so after an eMMC boot with an empty slot a card inserted later was met with
complete silence: its bus pads were never wired to the controller.

Own the mux and the pulls in the DT, mirroring the eMMC group. The values
are the vendor bootloader's card-present ones: function 0 (SDIO0)
everywhere, pull-up on CMD/D0-D3, pull-down on CLK. power-source is required
by cv1800_dt_node_to_map_post() and only feeds the pull-strength tables; the
bus pads live in the VDDIO_SD0 domain, which this board runs at 3.3V.
SD0_CD and SD0_PWR_EN are left out: the bootloader keeps them on SDIO0 in
both of its paths, and they belong to the VDDIO_EMMC domain, where a second
power-source would have to match the eMMC group's forever.

The slot's power switch (TOP_SD_PWRSW_CTRL, 0x030001f4) is beyond pinctrl's
reach, so this is only half the fix: the bootloader must leave the slot
powered, which the Armbian U-Boot patch
(0003-mmc-cvitek-always-power-and-mux-the-SD-slot-on-probe) now guarantees.
With both in place the SD pad setup no longer depends on whether a card
happened to sit in the slot at boot.
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts | 44 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
@@ -271,10 +271,54 @@ &saradc {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * Route the microSD pads ourselves instead of inheriting them. The vendor
+ * U-Boot (cvi_sdhci_probe() in drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c) checks card
+ * detect when it probes the SD host: with a card in the slot it muxes
+ * SD0_CLK/CMD/D0-D3 to the SDIO0 function and pulls CMD and the data lines
+ * up, with the slot empty it muxes the same pads to XGPIO, pulls them down,
+ * and opens the slot's power switch. Linux inherited whichever state the boot
+ * happened in, so after an eMMC boot with the slot empty a card inserted
+ * later was met with complete silence: the bus pads were not wired to the
+ * controller at all.
+ *
+ * This group restores the mux and the pulls of the card-present case. What it
+ * cannot restore is the power switch (TOP_SD_PWRSW_CTRL, 0x030001f4) - no
+ * mainline driver knows that register, and power-source below only feeds the
+ * pinconf pull-strength tables, it programs nothing - so the bootloader still
+ * has to leave the slot powered; the Armbian U-Boot patch does exactly that.
+ *
+ * SD0_CD and SD0_PWR_EN are not in the group: the vendor code keeps them on
+ * the SDIO0 function in both of its paths, and they sit in the VDDIO_EMMC
+ * power domain, where a second power-source would forever have to agree with
+ * the eMMC group's.
+ */
+&pinctrl {
+	sdhci0_cfg: sdhci0-cfg {
+		sd0-bus-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_SD0_CMD, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_SD0_D0, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_SD0_D1, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_SD0_D2, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_SD0_D3, 0)>;
+			bias-pull-up;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+
+		sd0-clk-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_SD0_CLK, 0)>;
+			bias-pull-down;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
 &sdhci0 {
 	bus-width = <4>;
 	no-1-8-v;
 	disable-wp;
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&sdhci0_cfg>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
-- 
Armbian

